Create a digital illustration for a Synthwave 1980s album cover. Show a futuristic sports car driving toward a glowing neon sunset over a geometric grid. Use bright pink, purple, and blue values against deep, dark shadows to create strong contrast. Create the illusion of space by making the car large in the foreground while the grid and mountains become smaller and fade into the background. Use diagonal laser lines and motion blur to suggest time and motion and make the car feel like it is moving forward. Use a consistent neon color palette for unity, but add one unexpected circular shape in the sky for variety. Use asymmetrical balance, with the large car on one side and the glowing sun on the other. Make the car the largest element so the scale creates a clear visual hierarchy and draws attention to it first.
Value: Value is used to create dark and light modes in an artwork, which creates contrast in the colors. If you look at my album cover, you see bright neon colors of the sun creating contrast on the road and deep, dark shadows of the car, which makes it feel like the 1980s era. 
Space: Space creates distance, and the car gridlines into the distance seem deep and flat. If you look at my album cover, the size of the car seems large on the ground, while the mountains, sky, and city seem smaller.
Unity: Unity means staying together, and my album cover design feels connected because of the colors. The neon colors of the image create a consistent style. 
Balance: Balance is when the parts of the design feel even and distributed. The mountains, the big car on the left, and the bright neon sun and the city on the right create balance in the poster. 
Rhythm: Rhythm is created by repeating gridlines, shapes, and colors, creating a movement towards the sun and the light streak, grabbing the attention of the viewers and feeling energetic.
